        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In Lessons 2 and 3, we saw why that first definition doesn't work: the subjects of many sentences are not doers. Here, for example, the subject is an action: The explosion was loud. Here it is a quality: Correctness is not writing's highest virtue. Here it is just a grammatical placeholder: It was a dark and stormy night.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                117,
                784,
                244
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 2,
            "global_block_id": 2,
            "global_group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    117.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    117.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    244.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    244.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "But also flawed is that second definition: a subject is what a sentence is about. It is flawed because, often, a sentence's topic is stated elsewhere than in the grammatical subject.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                247,
                783,
                321
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 3,
            "global_block_id": 3,
            "global_group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    247.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
